zero -- the failure mode that design rule 1 (orthogonalise against the linear
terms) exists to prevent. Data with independent \code{X} and \code{Z} cannot exercise
that rule at all.

At \code{confounding = 0} the columns of \code{X} are independent standard normals and
ordinary least squares of \code{y} on \code{X} is unbiased for \code{beta}. As \code{confounding}
rises, naive OLS becomes visibly biased. Both regimes are useful: the first
is the null case, the second is the reason for the package.
